    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the slope of the line that is perpendicular to the given line \\(2x + 3y = 6\\), we first need to determine the slope of the given line. The given line is in slope-intercept form \\(y = mx + b\\), where \\(m\\) is the slope. \n\nStarting with the equation:\n\\[2x + 3y = 6\\]\n\nWe solve for \\(y\\):\n\\[3y = -2x + 6\\]\n\\[y = -\\frac{2}{3}x + 2\\]\n\nFrom the equation \\(y = -\\frac{2}{3}x + 2\\), we see that the slope \\(m\\) of the given line is \\(-\\frac{2}{3}\\).\n\nNext, we need to find the slope of the line that is perpendicular to the given line. The slope of a line that is perpendicular to another line is the negative reciprocal of the original line's slope. Therefore, the slope of the line perpendicular to the given line is:\n\\[-\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)} = \\frac{1}{\\frac{2}{3}} = \\frac{3}{2}\\]\n\nThus, the slope of the line that is perpendicular to the line \\(2x + 3y = 6\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{3}{2}}\\).",
